of independent runs).\n- Number of benchmark molecules: 8 (baseline handful) up to ~200 (hundreds).\n- Simulation length per molecule: e.g., 100 ns production per independent run, 3-5 independent runs per molecule for statistics.\n\nSo compute_axis = number of benchmark molecules (from 8 to ~200). That's the natural compute axis: \"more compute buys more benchmark molecules\". The option says \"More compute buys more benchmark molecules ... and longer trajectories per molecule.\" So two axes but the primary is number of molecules.\n\nLet me set compute_axis.quantity = \"benchmark molecules scored\" unit = \"molecules\", levels = [8, 50, 200] or similar.\n\nActually let me think about cost. \n\nThroughput for a small organic liquid MD: a system with, say, a few hundred molecules of C10-C50. For Green-Kubo viscosity, you need long trajectories (correlation time for viscosity can be long, especially for viscous fluids; for mineral oil viscosity ~10-100 mPa·s, correlation times can be tens to hundreds of ps, so you need many ns). Thermal conductivity GK also needs long runs.\n\nLet me estimate: A box of ~10,000-30,000 atoms. GROMACS on an A100/H100 with a TraPPE/OPLS force field (no charges or simple charges), 2 fs timestep with constraints. Throughput maybe 100-500 ns/day on a single modern GPU. Let me find a citable number.\n\nGROMACS official benchmarks: On their website, there are benchmark results. For example, a ~30k atom system... Actually let me search.\n\nAlternative: Use a citable paper on MD transport properties of hydrocarbons/esters with reported simulation lengths and GPU usage.\n\nLet me search now.\n\nActually, I realize I should also think about the \"restructuring\" part of the schema. The schema has a lot of restructuring fields (sequential_part, method, condition, condition_phase, baseline_sequential, baseline_copies). This is about whether the computation has a sequential chain that can be parallelized. For this benchmark, the work is embarrassingly