.\n\nLet me decide: method = \"none\" because each molecule is independent and runs in parallel on separate GPUs; there's no step-by-step chain to restructure. sequential_part: \"Each molecule's viscosity and thermal conductivity come from one Green-Kubo trajectory, accumulating step after step over ~50 million steps.\" That describes the sequential part (the trajectory). method = \"none\" means the plan doesn't restructure it (can't parallelize time-evolution of a single trajectory cheaply), just runs many molecules in parallel.\n\nActually wait, re-read: \"restructuring.sequential_part is the part of this computation that one answer waits on step after step, and how many steps; method is why those steps need not run in order and how your plan computes them at the same time instead, or 'none' if the plan only runs independent work side by side or splits each step across GPUs.\"\n\nSo if the plan only runs independent work side by side (molecules) or splits each step across GPUs, method = \"none\". This is exactly our case: each molecule is independent. So method = \"none\". sequential_part still should describe the sequential chain (the trajectory). Good.\n\ncompute_axis = number of benchmark molecules (the number of runs the answer needs) — this fits \"the number of runs the answer needs\" phrasing.\n\nNow, let me think about the actual plan structure and cost.\n\nPlan:\n- Phase A (pilot/validation, cheap): Pick a small set of anchor molecules with well-established measured viscosity and thermal conductivity (e.g., n-decane C10, n-hexadecane C16, n-docosane C22, maybe an ester like methyl oleate or ethyl hexanoate). Run MD with TraPPE-UA (or OPLS-AA) force field in GROMACS, Green-Kubo for viscosity and thermal conductivity, compare to NIST/DIPPR measured values. This anchors the protocol and gives per-molecule throughput (ns/day) and error vs measurement on a few molecules. This also measures the per-molecule cost and the error on known fluids, informing the full-scale.\n\n- Phase B (enlarged benchmark): Run the same protocol over N molecules (the compute axis), from 8 up to ~200, scoring against measured values (NIST/DIPPR). Report error distribution and ranking agreement (Spearman/Kendall rank correlation between MD and measurement for viscosity and thermal conductivity).\n\n- Phase C (analysis): error distribution, systematic bias vs random floor, ranking comparison.\n\nThe predictions: if_lever (more compute changes the answer): the MD error distribution and ranking agreement stabilize and reveal that MD ranks fluids correctly (Spearman rho > 0.9) OR reveals a systematic bias. if_not: the ranking error is a random floor that doesn't improve with more molecules; error is dominated by run-to-run noise / force-field randomness, capping ranking trust.\n\nHmm, need to be careful. The decision is about whether more benchmark molecules (compute) changes the answer (whether MD's ranking is trustworthy). Let me frame:\n\n- if_lever: With more molecules, MD's ranking vs measurement converges to a stable, high rank correlation (rho ≥ 0.9) and a tight error distribution → ranking trustworthy.\n- if_not: With more molecules, the rank correlation stays low/doesn't improve (rho < 0.9 or error floor) → random floor caps trust.\n\ndecision_rule: threshold on Spearman rank correlation between MD-predicted and measured viscosity over the benchmark set, e.g., rho ≥ 0.9 means trustworthy; below means floor.\n\nHmm, but \"if_lever\" and \"if_not\" are about whether more compute changes the answer. Let me re-read: \"predictions.if_lever and predictions.if_not are the outcomes you expect, on the same measured quantity, if more compute does or does not change the answer.\" And \"predictions.expected is the outcome you expect, if_lever, if_not or unsure.\"\n\nSo the measured quantity is e.g., the rank correlation rho between MD and measurement, or the error distribution spread. if_lever: more compute → rho stabilizes at high value (ranking is trustworthy, error is a correctable systematic bias). if_not: more compute → rho doesn't improve / error is a random floor (ranking can't be trusted beyond some accuracy).\n\ncompute_tested: does the test vary a quantity that more parallel compute buys? Yes — number of molecules (the compute axis). compute_tested = \"yes\".\n\nNow cost. Let me get numbers.\n\nLet me now actually do the research.\n\nKey citable facts I want:\n1. GPU throughput (ns/day) for MD of organic liquids (small system) on A100/H100. GROMACS performance docs or a paper.\n2. Typical simulation length for Green-Kubo viscosity/thermal conductivity of hydrocarbons (ns). Papers on TraPPE viscosity of n-alkanes.\n3. Measured viscosity/thermal conductivity database: NIST WebBook / DIPPR 801; number of compounds available.\n4. Force field: TraPPE-UA or OPLS-AA for hydrocarbons/esters.\n5. Commercial single-phase immersion coolants and their chemistry (mineral oil, esters) — to justify benchmark composition.\n6. Typical error of MD viscosity vs experiment (% ) — to set the \"error floor\" context.\n\nLet me search.\n\nI'll batch searches.\n\nLet me write out search queries:\n1. websearch \"GROMACS molecular dynamics viscosity Green-Kubo n-alkanes TraPPE simulation nanoseconds\"\n2. websearch \"molecular dynamics thermal conductivity Green-Kubo hydrocarbon ester nanoseconds GPU A100 throughput\"\n3. websearch \"immersion cooling single-phase dielectric fluid mineral oil synthetic ester Midel viscosity\"\n4. websearch \"NIST WebBook viscosity thermal conductivity database number of compounds\"\n\nLet me start.\n\nActually, let me also confirm lambda pricing page to have a citable URL. I have the price sheet in the prompt; I can cite it as source with URL https://lambda.ai/pricing. I might also fetch it to confirm. Let me fetch it too.\n\nLet me start with parallel searches and a fetch.\n\nI'll be mindful of the 45-minute limit; but web research should be quick.\n\nLet me go."
