    "reasoning_content": "Good progress. I now have several useful sources:\n\n1. NIST Messerly 2018 paper (pub_id=926488): \"Equilibrium molecular dynamics simulations are analyzed using the Green-Kubo method for viscosity characterization. Simulations are performed for linear alkanes with two to...\" and \"TAMie) provide roughly 10 % accuracy for linear alkanes, while deviations are between 20 and 50 % for TraPPE-UA. For branched alkanes... TraPPE-UA has deviations of approximately...\" This gives me the force-field error magnitude (10-50%) for viscosity of alkanes. Very relevant to \"error floor\". Let me fetch the full text or get more precise quotes.\n\n2. GROMACS throughput: NVIDIA HPC application performance page gives \"GROMACS [ADH Dodec] | ns/day | ADH Dodec | yes | 333 | 1,997 | 2,990 | 5,933\" for some GPU configs (probably per-GPU or multi-GPU). Also the NHR@FAU slides give GROMACS GPU benchmarks in ns/day on H100/A100 for various systems. System sizes unknown in the search excerpt, but the slide shows e.g., A100 System 1: 267.48 ns/day, System 2: 691.67, etc. But these are water/biomolecular systems of specific sizes. The \"ADH Dodec\" (alcohol dehydrogenase in dodecahedron? actually \"ADH Dodec\" is a GROMACS benchmark system ~ 100k atoms) gives 333 ns/day CPU-only, and up to ~6000 ns/day on 4x GB200. Hmm.\n\nActually a more relevant throughput for organic liquids: small liquid boxes (~5-30k atoms). The GROMACS manual says \"~10000 atoms/GPU\" scaling limit. For a 10k-30k atom united-atom hydrocarbon box, on an H100 we'd expect maybe 200-1000 ns/day. Let me get a concrete citable number.\n\nThe Messerly NIST paper itself might state simulation lengths and resources. Let me fetch its full text. Actually it says GPU support disabled (CPU GROMACS). That's older (2018).\n\nLet me think about what I actually need a citable throughput for: the per-molecule GPU cost. I need a ns/day figure for a small hydrocarbon/ester liquid on an A100 or H100.\n\nOptions:\n- NVIDIA HPC application performance page: \"GROMACS [ADH Dodec] | ns/day | ... 333 | 1,997 | 2,990 | 5,933\" — but ADH Dodec is ~100k atoms, not our small liquid. The columns are CPU-only (AMD Genoa 9684X), 2x GB200, 4x GB200. So 1997 ns/day on 2x GB200 = ~1000 ns/day per GB200 for a 100k-atom system. That's a proxy.\n\n- NHR@FAU slides: GPU benchmarks with system sizes not shown in excerpt. Could fetch to get atom counts. But this is a PDF/slides.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. For cost estimation, I can be more robust by:\n- Using a conservative ns/day (e.g., 100 ns/day per GPU for a ~10k atom liquid on A100), and clearly mark as PROXY/DESIGN.\n- Or better: fetch a specific recent paper on MD transport of hydrocarbons/esters that reports ns/day or simulation length + hardware.\n\nLet me search more specifically. Also I want:\n- Number of benchmark molecules possible: DIPPR 801 has 1700+ chemicals; NIST TDE has many. But I need \"C10-C50 hydrocarbons and esters with measured viscosity and thermal conductivity\". I can estimate: n-alkanes C10-C50 (~40), branched alkanes (~hundreds), esters (methyl/ethyl esters of fatty acids C10-C22, triglycerides, etc.). Realistically a few hundred. This is a DESIGN choice for levels [8, 50, 200].\n\n- The known error of force-field viscosity: Messerly 2018 gives 10-50% for TraPPE-UA on alkanes. Good citable number.\n\n- Green-Kubo simulation length needed: The hGK arxiv paper says \"traditional GK framework requires tens of nanoseconds to microseconds of sampling for convergence\" for viscous systems. For n-alkanes (low viscosity ~1 mPa·s), correlation times are shorter, so 10-50 ns may suffice. But for mineral oil / esters (10-100 mPa·s), longer. Let me find a paper on viscosity of n-alkanes via GK with specific ns.\n\nThe Messerly 2018 paper likely reports simulation lengths (e.g., 10-100 ns, multiple independent runs). Let me fetch its PDF text.\n\nLet me also get the NIST TDE / NIST WebBook to cite \"measured values\" as th